    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Starting from his father's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jagir">jagir</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pune">Pune</a>, Shivaji took Bijapuri forts from 1647 and built a state between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan-sultanates">Deccan sultanates</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughal Empire</a>.</li><li>He alternated between war and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassalage</a> with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughals</a>, sacking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/surat">Surat</a> in 1664 and 1670, submitting at Purandar in 1665 and escaping house arrest at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agra">Agra</a> in 1666.</li><li>Crowned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chhatrapati">Chhatrapati</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raigad">Raigad</a> in 1674 despite the objection of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmins</a> that he was not a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kshatriya">Kshatriya</a>, he has since been claimed by reformers, nationalists and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hindutva">Hindutva</a> activists in turn.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Shivaji replaced Persian with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marathi">Marathi</a> at his court and had his official Raghunath Pandit compile the Rājavyavahārakośa (1677), a thesaurus replacing Persian and Arabic terms of state with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> ones.</p></aside>
    </section>

