    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1647</span><span class="rf-text">Shivaji, aged 16, captures <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/torna-fort">Torna Fort</a> and uses its treasure to build <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajgad">Rajgad</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1659</span><span class="rf-text">He kills the Bijapuri general Afzal Khan at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pratapgad">Pratapgad</a> and defeats his army</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1664</span><span class="rf-text">Shivaji sacks the Mughal port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/surat">Surat</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1665</span><span class="rf-text">By the Treaty of Purandar he gives up 23 forts and becomes a Mughal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1666</span><span class="rf-text">Placed under house arrest at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agra">Agra</a>, he escapes with his son Sambhaji</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1674</span><span class="rf-text">Shivaji is crowned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chhatrapati">Chhatrapati</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raigad">Raigad</a> Fort</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1677</span><span class="rf-text">He invades <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnataka">Karnataka</a> and takes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vellore">Vellore</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gingee">Gingee</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1680</span><span class="rf-text">Shivaji dies at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raigad">Raigad</a> Fort</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="early">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Early Life</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Shivaji was born at the hill-fort of Shivneri near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/junnar">Junnar</a>, in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pune">Pune</a> district; scholars disagree on the date, and the Government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharashtra">Maharashtra</a> marks 19 February as Shivaji Jayanti. He was named after a local goddess, Shivai Devi. His father, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shahaji-bhonsle">Shahaji Bhonsle</a>, was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha</a> general who served the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan-sultanates">Deccan sultanates</a>, and his mother, Jijabai, was the daughter of Lakhuji Jadhavrao of Sindhkhed, a Mughal-aligned sardar claiming descent from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yadava">Yadava</a> kings of Devagiri. His grandfather Maloji had been a general of the Ahmadnagar Sultanate and held deshmukhi rights over Pune and other districts.</p><p>Power in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a> was then shared between the sultanates of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bijapur">Bijapur</a>, Ahmednagar and Golkonda and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughal Empire</a>. Shahaji changed his loyalty often but kept his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jagir">jagir</a> at Pune and his small army. While the Mughals pursued him, Shivaji and Jijabai moved from fort to fort. In 1636 Shahaji entered Bijapur's service, obtained Poona as a grant and, posted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengaluru">Bangalore</a>, left Dadoji Kondadeo as its administrator; Shivaji and his mother settled there. The scholar James Laine states that Jijabai, conscious of her Yadava heritage, imbued her son with the dream of re-establishing a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> kingdom. When Kondadeo died in 1647, Shivaji took over the administration.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rise">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Rise</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In 1647, aged 16, Shivaji took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/torna-fort">Torna Fort</a> by stratagem or bribery during the illness of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> Mohammed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adil-shah">Adil Shah</a> and used its treasure to build <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajgad">Rajgad</a>, his seat for over a decade. He then took Purandar, Kondhana and Chakan and the town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalyan">Kalyan</a> in the Konkan. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bijapur">Bijapur</a> responded by imprisoning his father in 1648, and from 1649 to 1655 Shivaji consolidated quietly. In 1656, under controversial circumstances, he killed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha</a> feudatory Chandrarao More and seized the valley of Javali, and he subdued other Deshmukh families by marriage alliances, dealings with village <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/patil">Patils</a> or force.</p><p>In 1657 Bijapur sent the veteran general Afzal Khan against him. On 10 November 1659 the two met in a hut below <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pratapgad">Pratapgad</a> fort; Shivaji wore armour and hid a bagh nakh, and in a struggle known mainly from Maratha legends he disembowelled Khan. In the Battle of Pratapgarh that followed more than 3,000 Bijapuri soldiers were killed, and the captives were sent home with gifts. Besieged at Panhala by Siddi Jauhar in 1660, Shivaji escaped by night while Baji Prabhu Deshpande and 300 soldiers held the pass later renamed Pavan Khind, and he surrendered the fort on 22 September 1660.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="deeds">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">What They Did</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughals</a> Shivaji raided <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/junnar">Junnar</a> in 1657, carrying off 300,000 hun. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurangzeb">Aurangzeb</a>'s uncle Shaista Khan occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pune">Pune</a>, Shivaji with 400 men attacked his mansion on the night of 5 April 1663, wounding him; the general's son and several wives, servants and soldiers were killed. In 1664 he sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/surat">Surat</a>, taking plunder exceeding Rs 10 million. Pressed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jai-singh-i">Jai Singh I</a>, he signed the Treaty of Purandar on 11 June 1665, giving up 23 forts and becoming a Mughal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a>. Summoned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agra">Agra</a> in 1666 and placed under house arrest, he escaped on 17 August, by the famous story hidden in a basket of sweets; Stewart N. Gordon finds no contemporary evidence for it and thinks he likely bribed the guards.</p><p>Peace followed until 1670, when he recovered much of the ceded land, sacked Surat again and defeated a Mughal force at Vani-Dindori. On 6 June 1674 he was crowned at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raigad">Raigad</a>, taking the titles <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chhatrapati">Chhatrapati</a> and Shakakarta. He then campaigned in the south, made a treaty with Golkonda in 1677 and invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnataka">Karnataka</a> with 30,000 cavalry and 40,000 infantry, taking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vellore">Vellore</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gingee">Gingee</a>. He ruled through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashta-pradhan">Ashta Pradhan</a> council of eight ministers, built the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha</a> navy with its headquarters at Sindhudurg, and by Jadunath Sarkar's estimate held some 240-280 forts at his death.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="opponents">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Opponents &amp; Critics</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Shivaji's enemies were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bijapur">Bijapur</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughal Empire</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siddis">Siddis</a> of Janjira and at times the European companies. He plundered the English factory at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajapur">Rajapur</a> after its men helped bombard Panhala and never paid the indemnity the English sought. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurangzeb">Aurangzeb</a> and his generals called him a "Mountain Rat", and in Mughal eyes he was only a rebel <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zamindar">zamindar</a>; Mughal writers called him simply "<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a>", and one recorded his death as "the infidel went to Hell". In 1677 he was defeated by Chikkadevaraja Wodeyar after plundering <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srirangapatna">Srirangapatna</a>. At Purandar he wrote letters to Aurangzeb asking forgiveness and calling himself "the meanest of life-devoting slaves".</p><p>His coronation was delayed because <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmins</a> of his court held that kingship belonged to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kshatriya">Kshatriya</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varna">varna</a> and that he, of a line of village headmen, was not one. Gaga Bhatt of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varanasi">Varanasi</a>, whom Shivaji is said to have bribed, produced a genealogy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sisodia">Sisodia</a> descent, which historical evidence suggests is tenuous or invented. Two Brahmins held that his raids had killed Brahmins, cows, women and children, and he paid Rs 8,000 for cleansing. Jadunath Sarkar was criticised for calling the killing of Chandrarao More and his clan a murder. Gijs Kruijtzer argues that the foundations of modern Hindu-Muslim communalism were laid in the interplay between Shivaji and Aurangzeb.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="last">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Last Years</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The succession troubled Shivaji's last years. In 1678 he confined his eldest son Sambhaji to Panhala Fort, for an addiction to sensual pleasures or for violating a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmin</a> woman; Sambhaji escaped, defected to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughals</a> and fought against his father at Bhupalgarh before returning, unrepentant, to confinement. In late March 1680 Shivaji fell ill with fever and dysentery, and he died at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raigad">Raigad</a> Fort around 3-5 April 1680, aged 50. British records give bloody flux, a Portuguese work anthrax and the Sabhasad Bakhar fever; allegations that his wife Soyarabai poisoned him are doubted by later scholars. His childless eldest surviving wife, Putalabai, committed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sati-practice">sati</a> on his funeral pyre.</p><p>Soyarabai and several ministers installed her ten-year-old son Rajaram on 21 April 1680. Sambhaji seized Raigad after killing its commander, took control on 18 June and formally ascended the throne on 20 July; Rajaram, his mother and his wife were imprisoned, and Soyarabai was executed for conspiracy in October. The military organisation Shivaji had built, of Maval infantry, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha</a> cavalry, forts and a coastal navy, lasted until the end of the Maratha Empire, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gingee">Gingee</a> later served as Rajaram's capital.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Contemporary English, French, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a>, Portuguese and Italian writers admired Shivaji's exploits; François Bernier recorded that he spared the Capuchin mission at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/surat">Surat</a>, and Jadunath Sarkar wrote that his chivalry to women won the admiration even of hostile critics such as Khafi Khan. Many modern commentators deem his religious policy tolerant: he endowed Muslim institutions, had Muslim commanders and a Muslim admiral, Darya Sarang, and urged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurangzeb">Aurangzeb</a> to respect <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> places as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akbar">Akbar</a> had. Early bakhars present him as an almost divine Hindu king, and scholars hold they must be checked against other sources.</p><p>About two centuries after his death <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jyotirao-phule">Jyotirao Phule</a> portrayed him in 1869 as a hero of the shudras and dalits. From 1895 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bal-gangadhar-tilak">Bal Gangadhar Tilak</a> held an annual festival for his birthday, and M. G. Ranade's Rise of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha</a> Power (1900) saw in him the beginning of modern nation-building. He is a national hero in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, above all in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharashtra">Maharashtra</a>; the Shiv Sena, formed in 1966, bears his name, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mumbai">Mumbai</a>'s main station, airport and museum were renamed for him. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hindutva">Hindutva</a> activists are noted for presenting him as a Hindu king who fought Muslim rulers, contrary to accounts of his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">caste</a> and secular values. His depiction stays contested: James W. Laine's 2003 book was banned in Maharashtra in 2004 and the Bhandarkar Oriental Research Institute attacked, before the courts lifted the ban.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Shivaji built the Maratha Kingdom from his father's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jagir">jagir</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pune">Pune</a>, taking forts from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bijapur">Bijapur</a>, fighting and serving the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughals</a> in turn, and having himself crowned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chhatrapati">Chhatrapati</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raigad">Raigad</a> in 1674. His <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> warfare, forts, navy and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marathi">Marathi</a> court outlived him, and his memory has been claimed by social reformers, nationalists and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hindutva">Hindutva</a> activists.</em></p>

